          <details class="group p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-50/70 dark:bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-200/70 dark:border-zinc-800/70">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm sm:text-base text-zinc-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              <span>Can an online IQ test qualify someone for Mensa membership?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
              No unproctored online test qualifies directly for official high-IQ society membership. Formal admission requires either sitting for an in-person, proctored admissions battery administered by Mensa or a certified psychologist, or submitting verified transcripts from recognized clinical or academic standardized examinations.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-50/70 dark:bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-200/70 dark:border-zinc-800/70">
            <summary class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm sm:text-base text-zinc-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              <span>Who has the highest IQ ever recorded in history?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
              Historical figures like child prodigy William James Sidis (speculated 250–300) and Marilyn vos Savant (recorded 228 on a childhood Binet-ratio scale) are commonly cited. Among living mathematicians, Terence Tao has estimated scores around 225-230. However, modern adult deviation IQ tests (WAIS-IV) possess a statistical ceiling of 160 (+4.00 standard deviations). Psychometricians consider scores above 160 to be statistical extrapolations rather than standardized experimental measures.
            </p>
